  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA frontal boundary will approach and stall out over the \nforecast area today. On the north side of the boundary, the air \nmass will moderate a little with temperatures in the 70s and dew\npoints falling a bit into the 60s and maybe upper 50s. However,\nthe south side of this boundary will remain humid and \nrelatively unsettled with highs near 80 degrees and dew points \nin the 70s. This air mass will remain favorable for more shower \nand storm development this afternoon and evening. The bulk of \nthe development should be south of the forecast area where \ngreater instability will build. However, some activity will \ndevelop across the southern portion of the forecast area with a \nmid-level shortwave moving through the region. With a limited \nthermodynamic environment and storms should progress with the \nshortwave, severe weather and flooding hazards remain very low \nat this time. The surface boundary will push south for Thursday \nnight into Friday with high pressure entering the region, which \nwill allow for 36 hours of dry, comfortable weather.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUnsettled weather will return to the Ohio Valley for Saturday \nas a mid-level shortwave will move through the Ohio and \nTennessee Valleys behind Friday's high pressure system. This \nwave will allow for a warm front to lift back over the area, \nallowing temperatures back into the 80s and dew points back in \nthe 60s to lower 70s. This shortwave will provide some lift for \nshower and thunderstorm development, especially on Saturday \nafternoon and evening. With activity currently expected during \npeak heating hours, there is a low end threat for a stronger \nthunderstorm, as the atmosphere may be unstable enough to allow \nfor some gusty winds to materialize. However, details may change\nover the next couple of forecast cycles, especially if timing \nis faster or slower or if the wave is less aggressive in lifting\nthe warm front north. Activity could linger into Sunday with a \ncold front trying to approach from the northwest, extending from\na low pressure system moving through Canada. Return flow is \naggressive across the region early next week and may try to keep\nthis feature north, as the main frontal support quickly departs\nto the northeast. If the front doesn't reach the area, the area\nmay end up staying dry with activity to the north.",
